+# NABLA — corrected specification (one language)
+
+A tiny language in which a program can differentiate its own source and update
+it *while it runs*. The update is an ordinary opcode (`GRAD`) reached by a normal
+program counter, not an external training loop. The loss is an ordinary cell the
+program computes, addressable and overwritable like any other — so objective
+hacking is native, not bolted on.
+
+## Grammar
+
+```
+program    := { line }
+line       := [ label ":" ] [ instr | directive ] [ "#" comment ]
+directive  := "PARAM" name number          # declare a trainable operand immediate
+instr      := "SET" dst operand
+            | "ADD" dst operand operand     # dst = a + b
+            | "SUB" dst operand operand     # dst = a - b
+            | "MUL" dst operand operand     # dst = a * b
+            | "OUT" operand                 # append value to output
+            | "IN"  dst                     # read next input value
+            | "PTR" ("+" | "-")             # data pointer += / -= 1
+            | "JMP" label
+            | "JZ"  operand label           # jump if round(value)==0
+            | "JNZ" operand label           # jump if round(value)!=0
+            | "GRAD"                         # in-band gradient update (see below)
+            | "HALT"
+dst        := name | "*"                    # "*" is the cell at the data pointer
+operand    := number | name | "~"name | "*" # ~name = a trainable PARAM
+```
+
+Variables (`name`) are cells on an unbounded integer-addressed tape, default 0.
+`*` is the cell under the data pointer `DP` (moved by `PTR`). PARAMs are the only
+continuous, differentiable part of a program.
+
+## Execution
+
+The interpreter's ONLY external inputs are `(source, input, max_steps)`. The PC
+starts at 0 and advances by 1 except on taken jumps. Everything a demo needs —
+`eta`, the target, how the loss is built, how many times to iterate, and where
+`GRAD` sits — is written in the program. Convention: cell `E` holds `eta`, cell
+`L` holds the loss; both are set by ordinary instructions.
+
+`GRAD` semantics (the only differentiable opcode): reverse-mode differentiate the
+current value of cell `L` with respect to every PARAM it depends on; for each such
+PARAM, `p := p - E * dL/dp`; then detach the graph. The window is **W=0** — a later
+`GRAD` does not differentiate through an earlier `GRAD`'s update. The update *mask*
+is exactly the set of PARAMs `L` depends on; the program controls it by how it
+wires the loss (dataflow *is* the mask). No opcode identity is ever differentiated:
+under hard execution the structural gradient is 0 almost everywhere, so all
+learning lives in operands (the operand-cell law).
+
+## Export and the hard interpreter
+
+`export(params, grid)` rounds each trained PARAM to `grid` and freezes it. A
+*separate* hard interpreter (`run_hard`) executes with plain floats and treats
+`GRAD` as a no-op; running an exported program there is the deployed, non-learning
+machine. `rounding_gap = soft_loss − hard_loss`, measured (not asserted): for the
+learning demo the soft param 2.79384 exports to 2.8, gap ≈ 0.0025.
+
+## The three programs (same parser, same dispatch loop, text-only differences)
+
+| program        | what it shows                    | trace (verified in `test_nabla.py`)                 |
+|----------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
+| `learning.nbl` | useful gradient learning         | `a_n = 3(1−0.8^n)`: 0, 0.6, 1.08, 1.464 → 2.8; loss 9 → 0 |
+| `hack.nbl`     | objective hacking (endogenous L) | one extra line `SET T R0`; loss ≡ 0, `a` frozen, target 3 never reached |
+| `erasure.nbl`  | differentiable self-erasure      | `g_n = 0.4^n`: 1, 0.4, 0.16 → 0; exports to an inert program |
+
+## Power ledger (claims that the tests actually back)
+
+- **Discrete core is Turing-complete.** The ISA has crisp branching (`JZ`/`JNZ`/
+  `JMP`) and unbounded memory (`PTR` over an unbounded tape). `compile_bf` reduces
+  Brainfuck — a known universal machine — into NABLA, and
+  `test_turing_completeness_bf_reduction` asserts NABLA output equals a reference
+  Brainfuck run (including a program that reverses its input). This is the *only*
+  basis on which TC is claimed.
+- **The executed differentiable object is bounded.** A single run with GRAD is a
+  finite, piecewise-analytic map of the PARAMs over ≤ `max_steps` instructions; it
+  is not itself a super-Turing device. Keeping export on a grid prevents a
+  surviving real operand from turning the machine into a BSS/super-Turing one.
+- **No claim of unsimulability**, and no higher-order (W≥1) claim: the interpreter
+  is W=0 and every trace above is at W=0.
+
+## Removed vs. the earlier drafts (unused features deleted)
+
+Soft opcode blending, the `softmax`/`tau` execution path, three-way STRUCTURAL vs
+DATA typing, the `kind=` loss selector, the `grad_cell` argument, the operand-`mask`
+argument, and the Python-side `passes` loop are all gone. Loops are in-language
+(`JNZ`), output is an opcode (`OUT`), and there is one dispatch loop for all three
+demos. Nothing here is claimed beyond what `python3 test_nabla.py` reproduces.
+```
